 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of iterations. Each iteration requires one field function evaluation.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. Only a few scalar and vector variables are stored.
-*   **Performance:** The bisection method is robust and guaranteed to converge if the function is continuous and the root is bracketed. However, it is generally slower than the Secant method or False Position method, which use linear interpolation to estimate the root location more aggressively. [S2]
-*   **Strengths:** Guaranteed convergence; simple to implement; no derivative information required.
-*   **Limitations:** Slower convergence rate compared to interpolation-based methods; requires a bracketing interval.
